    "_postman_id": "4d5e6f7a-8b9c-4d0e-1f2a-3b4c5d6e7f8a",
    "name": "LedgerOne - Inventory (Warehouse)",
    "description": "Inventory module (Warehouse) API collection (Create, Get, Update, List by Branch). Reflects the implementation exactly as shipped in apps/api/src/shared/inventory.\n\nDesign note (tenant-owned, X-Tenant-Id always required; header rewrite): identical posture to ProductCategory.postman_collection.json/Unit.postman_collection.json/Product.postman_collection.json — every /api/v1/inventory/* route sits behind jwt-auth.middleware.ts AND current-tenant.middleware.ts mounted with `{ rewriteHeaderAs: \"decimal\" }` (module-registry.ts), which overwrites `x-tenant-id` in place with the verified JWT claim before this module's own tenantIdHeaderSchema ever inspects it — so a missing/malformed X-Tenant-Id does not itself fail validation here either. No dedicated 'missing/malformed X-Tenant-Id' failure case is included for the same reason.\n\nKNOWN GAP, flagged explicitly (unlike Product Category/Unit/Product, this one has no in-collection workaround): Warehouse's `branchUuid` is a cross-module reference (FK-002/WHS-001) to Organization's `branches.uuid`, and there is no way to discover or create a valid one through THIS collection alone — Inventory's own Business layer never validates branchUuid's existence (create-warehouse.service.ts's own header comment: 'this Business layer depends only on IInventoryRepository, never on Organization's own repository'), so there is no Inventory endpoint to chain from. The `{{branchUuid}}` collection variable defaults to an empty string and MUST be filled in manually with a real Branch uuid from your local dev database before running the 'Success' requests below — create one via Organization's own collection at engineering/testing/organization/Organization.postman_collection.json (its Branch folder), then paste that Branch's uuid into this collection's `{{branchUuid}}` variable (or the shared LedgerOne.postman_environment.json's own `branchUuid` entry). This collection deliberately does not chain to Organization's collection to run it, mirroring this module's own established precedent (Product.postman_collection.json's Setup folder only creates fixtures Inventory itself owns — Product Category/Unit — never a cross-module Organization fixture).\n\nDesign note (name AND code uniqueness, both scoped per Branch, Ch.37.8): unlike Product's single productCode uniqueness rule, Warehouse enforces BOTH warehouseCode uniqueness and name uniqueness, independently, both scoped to the same Branch (create-warehouse.service.ts checks `findWarehouseByCode` then a `listWarehousesByBranch` name scan). This collection tests both violations separately.\n\nThere is no delete endpoint for Warehouse, so a collection-level pre-request script derives a `{{warehouseSuffix}}` from the current timestamp (once per Newman invocation) appended to every created warehouseCode/name, avoiding cross-run collisions.\n\nRun order for a full pass, top to bottom: `{{branchUuid}}` must be manually populated before running this collection at all; {{warehouseUuid}}/{{warehouseCode}}/{{warehouseName}} must be set (by 'Create Primary Warehouse - Success') before the Duplicate/Get/Update/List requests below run.",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
